p('Ballast / private research / 7 November 2026','small');p('Changing volatility,<br/>finite price moments.<br/>Still research.','title')
p('The annual conditional-volatility extension is built and tested. It fixes the mathematical price-moment blocker through an explicit variance ceiling. Weekly forecasts improve against iid, but annual scores are mixed and the portfolio evidence does not show a better strategy. The current default and funding limits are unchanged.')
p('What changed','h');p('Each asset and FX variance updates from the previous shock. The variance state stays between 0.05 and 9 times the past baseline variance. The ceiling is three times the baseline standard deviation, not a 9x return limit. Decay stays 0.94. These are declared research settings, not fitted market truths or an ordinary GARCH model.')
image('cap-fixture.png',153)
p('Illustrative hand fixture, NOT market data: a 15-baseline-SD shock at step3. The cap changes later volatility; it does not trim the shock or limit return/price support. Both paths use the same subsequent zero-shock fixture.','small')
p('Means, shrunk correlation, common Gaussian-mixture states, cash and the property latent-factor assumption remain as before. The selected model works through the installed annual engine, optimizer, independent judge and cash ledger. It is opt-in research, not a new automatic dashboard choice.')
p('Finite moments are proved, not inferred from finite simulations','h');p('A bounded conditional variance gives a finite upper bound on the conditional exponential moment of every finite listed/FX/property exposure. Repeated conditioning extends that bound to 52 weeks and any finite horizon. Returns stay unbounded. This is not the iid exact moment formula, a stationarity proof, a tail-calibration certificate or protection from floating-point overflow.')
